Overview Group revenue is forecast to grow from €136.9m (2023A) to €280.3m (2026LF) to €592.5m (2031E). Group GP margin declines modestly (56.2% → 53.6% → 51.7%) while EBITDA margin edges up (19.8% → 20.6% → 21.2%). Site-level performance is highly divergent: several sites show strong volume-driven growth with margin compression (often from mix or scale-up effects), others show margin expansion alongside growth or stabilisation. Expansion CAPEX is front-loaded in many cases, temporarily pressuring EBITDA-after-all-CAPEX; maintenance+quality (M+Q) CAPEX intensity is generally low-to-moderate. Format and customer concentration data (mix %) are the more reliable signals where absolute values in the format tables appear inconsistent with the EUR ’000s operational totals for certain sites (likely currency or scaling differences).
Duiven (1 & 2)
	•	Operational metrics: Revenue grows strongly (~8× from 2023A to 2031E: €10.1m → €42.0m → €80.3m), rising as % of group from 7.4% to a peak of 15% then 13.6%. GP margin compresses sharply (45.1% → 36.7% → 36.2%). EBITDA margin also declines (20.6% → 14.6% → 14.0%). Expansion CAPEX is elevated early then moderates in intensity (6.0% → 2.7% → 2.0%); M+Q intensity stays low (~1–1.6%). EBITDA after all CAPEX as % of EBITDA improves (62.7% → 72.8% → 77.1%).
	•	Format concentration: Heavily powder-oriented (stable ~67–72% of revenue). Gummies & bars rise as a share (14.3% → 28.5% → 30.3%). Capsules & tablets remain minor and decline in mix. Other and reconciliation fade.
	•	Customer concentration: Top-10 share jumps from 61% to 87% then eases to 68%. Customer 1 becomes dominant (0% → 57.6% → 44.4%).
	•	Overlay: High growth coincides with clear GP/EBITDA margin compression, consistent with a shift toward higher-volume powder/gummies mix (and/or pricing pressure or higher input costs at scale). Rising customer concentration (especially one large customer) adds risk that can weigh on pricing power and margins. Improving cash conversion after CAPEX reflects moderating expansion intensity as the site scales.
Enschede
	•	Operational metrics: Strong revenue growth (~8×: €7.3m → €25.0m → €58.5m), group share rising to ~10%. GP margin compresses (65.1% → 51.2% → 48.1%). EBITDA margin is relatively stable (19.6% → 18.6% → 19.2%). Expansion CAPEX intensity drops to zero in 2026LF then returns to ~2%; M+Q intensity falls then stabilises. EBITDA-after-all-CAPEX % improves sharply then moderates (65.4% → 93.5% → 80.7%).
	•	Format concentration: Dominated by capsules & tablets, with share rising further (80.1% → 89.4% → 89.8%). Powder, other, and minor formats decline in mix.
	•	Customer concentration: Top-10 share rises from 34% to 69% then eases to 54%. Customer 1 becomes the clear lead (rising to ~42% then ~33%).
	•	Overlay: Margin compression (especially GP) occurs alongside extreme focus on capsules & tablets and rising customer concentration. Scale benefits appear limited on the gross margin line; the more stable EBITDA margin and better post-CAPEX conversion suggest operating leverage and lower relative investment once growth is underway. High product and customer concentration increase risk if that format or key customer weakens.
Alkmaar
	•	Operational metrics: Solid revenue growth (~6.5×: €5.5m → €14.8m → €35.6m). GP margin stays high and stable-to-slightly up (65.3% → 67.1% → 66.3%). EBITDA margin compresses then stabilises at a lower level (38.3% → 27.7% → 27.4%). Notable expansion CAPEX spike in 2026LF (intensity 10.1%); M+Q intensity rises then falls. EBITDA-after-all-CAPEX % drops sharply in 2026LF (to 47%) then recovers.
	•	Format concentration: Almost entirely gels (85.3% → 79.9% → 78.4%), with “other” rising as residual share.
	•	Customer concentration: Top-10 remains high and stable (~73–79%).
	•	Overlay: High and stable GP margins align with a consistent gels-focused format. EBITDA margin compression and the temporary cash-flow dip track the expansion CAPEX cycle rather than a product-mix deterioration. Persistent high customer concentration is a structural feature but does not appear to have driven further margin erosion beyond the CAPEX effect.
Hamburg
	•	Operational metrics: Strong growth (~6.3×: €6.8m → €17.0m → €43.0m). GP margin rises then partially retraces (72.9% → 79.9% → 70.0%). EBITDA margin expands dramatically (7.3% → 39.2% → 38.0%). Low M+Q intensity; expansion intensity declines. EBITDA-after-all-CAPEX % recovers strongly (41% → 92% → 95%).
	•	Format concentration: Niche technologies dominate and increase to nearly 100% (77.3% → 99% → 99%).
	•	Customer concentration: Top-10 share rises from 55% to 83% then 76%.
	•	Overlay: The large EBITDA margin expansion is consistent with scale-up in a high-margin niche format and operating leverage. The later GP margin moderation may reflect mix normalisation or pricing. Rising customer concentration accompanies the growth but does not prevent margin improvement; post-CAPEX cash conversion becomes very strong.
Kumla
	•	Operational metrics: More moderate growth (~2.5×: €11.5m → €16.1m → €28.5m); group share declines. GP margin improves then eases (67.2% → 72.1% → 69.3%). EBITDA margin expands and holds (36.9% → 42.2% → 42.4%). Low CAPEX intensity overall. Very high EBITDA-after-CAPEX conversion (~93–98%).
	•	Format concentration: Capsules & tablets dominate and increase in share (approx. 79% → 90% → 91% based on available mix data).
	•	Customer concentration: Top-10 remains high (~73–84%).
	•	Overlay: Steady margin expansion (especially EBITDA) with moderate growth and a consistent, high-share capsules/tablets format points to operational efficiency and scale benefits within a stable product focus. High customer concentration is ongoing but has not prevented margin gains; low CAPEX supports strong cash conversion.
Sandholt & Sønderby
	•	Operational metrics: Moderate growth (~2.8×: €17.8m → €28.4m → €49.7m); group share declines. GP margin compresses (73.5% → 64.8% → 63.7%). EBITDA margin expands steadily (11.2% → 14.3% → 18.2%). High early expansion CAPEX (intensity 11.6% in 2023A) then moderates. EBITDA-after-all-CAPEX turns from negative to strong positive (–22% → 82% → 85%).
	•	Format concentration: Mixed (powder, gels, and large “other” share). Gels and powder gain relative prominence over time; “other” declines as a share of the total.
	•	Customer concentration: Top-10 share gradually rises (63% → 70% → 74%).
	•	Overlay: GP margin compression occurs alongside a shifting multi-format mix, while EBITDA margin expansion and the recovery in post-CAPEX conversion reflect operating leverage and the fading of heavy early expansion investment. Rising (but not extreme) customer concentration has not blocked the EBITDA improvement.
Barcelona
	•	Operational metrics: Relatively flat-to-modest growth after a dip (€22.1m → €16.5m → €27.0m); group share falls sharply. GP margin stable around 46–49%. EBITDA margin declines then stabilises lower (20.5% → 16.8% → 16.2%). Moderate CAPEX intensity. EBITDA-after-all-CAPEX conversion stays relatively solid but dips in 2026LF.
	•	Format concentration: Liquids dominate and increase then partially ease (61.8% → 82.8% → 79.2%). Capsules & tablets gain share over time; powder and “other” decline.
	•	Customer concentration: Top-10 share rises sharply and stays elevated (58% → 90% → 89%), with Customer 1 remaining very large.
	•	Overlay: Limited growth and lower EBITDA margins coincide with heavier reliance on liquids and much higher customer concentration. The stable GP margin suggests the liquids focus is not inherently low-margin, but lack of volume scale and high customer dependence limit operating leverage and overall profitability progression.
Madrid
	•	Operational metrics: Strong growth (~6.6×: €4.1m → €9.5m → €27.3m). GP margin rises then holds (62.0% → 66.6% → 65.9%). EBITDA margin expands (25.2% → 34.9% → 36.6%). Moderate and rising absolute CAPEX with stable-to-easing intensity. Strong and improving EBITDA-after-all-CAPEX conversion.
	•	Format concentration: Essentially 100% gummies & bars across the period.
	•	Customer concentration: Top-10 share rises sharply from a low base (16% → 80%) then eases to 60%.
	•	Overlay: Clean margin expansion with pure-play gummies focus and strong growth indicates successful scale-up in a higher-margin format. Temporary concentration spike does not prevent margin gains; the later moderation in concentration and strong cash conversion are positive.
Milan
	•	Operational metrics: Moderate growth (~2.4×: €4.7m → €6.2m → €11.4m). GP margin high and stable-to-slightly up (~75–78%). EBITDA margin expands (12.3% → 18.4% → 20.0%). Very high early expansion CAPEX intensity (25%) then normalises. EBITDA-after-all-CAPEX turns from deeply negative to solid.
	•	Format concentration: Niche technologies dominate (~98%).
	•	Customer concentration: Top-10 starts high (76%) then declines (73% → 52%).
	•	Overlay: Margin expansion (especially EBITDA) with a stable niche-format focus and declining customer concentration suggests improving operating leverage and reduced customer risk over time. The early CAPEX spike explains the temporary cash-flow pressure; subsequent recovery is consistent with a maturing site.
Bridgwater
	•	Operational metrics: Moderate growth (~2.4×: €10.2m → €13.2m → €24.9m). GP margin gradually improves (52.3% → 55.1% → 57.6%). EBITDA margin compresses then stabilises (29.6% → 23.0% → 23.7%). Expansion CAPEX intensity peaks in 2026LF. EBITDA-after-all-CAPEX conversion dips then recovers.
	•	Format concentration: Mixed (powder, gels, and significant “other”). Gels and powder remain important; absolute totals in the format table do not perfectly reconcile with operational revenue.
	•	Customer concentration: Top-10 stays moderate and fairly stable (~51–58%).
	•	Overlay: Improving GP margin with a diversified format mix is positive, but EBITDA margin compression and the mid-period CAPEX spike limit overall progression. Relatively low and stable customer concentration reduces one source of risk compared with more concentrated sites.
Los Angeles
	•	Operational metrics: Not operational in 2023A; then strong growth (€23.6m → €46.3m). GP margin expands then eases slightly (48% → 61.0% → 59.3%). EBITDA margin expands markedly (8.7% → 20.3% → 21.5%). Elevated M+Q and expansion CAPEX intensity. EBITDA-after-all-CAPEX conversion improves but remains lower than many European sites.
	•	Format concentration: 100% niche technologies once operational.
	•	Customer concentration: Extremely high (96% → 95% top-10), with Customer 1 dominant (~62–63%).
	•	Overlay: Clear margin expansion from a low base aligns with scale-up of a pure niche format. High absolute CAPEX and extreme customer concentration are the main offsets—growth and margin gains are real, but risk concentration and ongoing investment keep cash conversion more modest.
Wellma Europe
	•	Operational metrics: Very strong growth (~6.7×: €23.6m → €68.0m → €157.9m), becoming the largest contributor (group share rising to ~27%). GP margin gradually compresses (43.1% → 40.8% → 38.6%). EBITDA margin also declines (19.1% → 15.2% → 14.4%). Low CAPEX intensity. Very high EBITDA-after-CAPEX conversion (~94–96%).
	•	Format concentration: Capsules & tablets dominate and hold ~76–78%. Powder and gummies & bars fluctuate; liquids remain minor.
	•	Customer concentration: Top-10 share rises from 47% to 73% then eases to 61%.
	•	Overlay: High growth with progressive GP and EBITDA margin compression is consistent with a volume-driven capsules/tablets-led mix, possible pricing or cost pressures at scale, and rising (then partially easing) customer concentration. Low CAPEX intensity preserves strong cash conversion despite the margin dilution. This is the clearest large-scale example of growth accompanied by margin trade-offs.
Cross-site observations Sites with pure or increasingly dominant higher-margin formats (e.g., gels at Alkmaar, niche at Hamburg/Milan/LA, gummies at Madrid) more often show stable or expanding margins. Sites with heavy powder or liquids exposure, or rapid volume ramp-ups (Duiven, Enschede, Wellma Europe, parts of Sandholt), more often show GP/EBITDA compression. Customer concentration frequently rises during the growth phase and is highest at several high-growth or specialised sites; it correlates with risk but does not uniformly prevent margin gains where operating leverage or format quality is strong. Expansion CAPEX cycles are a recurring temporary drag on EBITDA-after-all-CAPEX, especially in 2023–2026 for growing sites. Overall, the group’s modest EBITDA margin improvement masks significant site-level divergence driven by mix, scale, concentration, and investment timing.
